{
  "description": "The four tiers of kid-hold-6 / grown-up-hold-5 and its US-first rating map (docs/adr/0028-rating-tiers.md). Written by hand from the ADR text, not computed with an independent reference. 'ratingMap': a rating string (null = absent) to a level of the shared ladder, 0 ALL, 1 = 7, 2 = 10, 3 = 13, 4 = 14, 5 = 16, 6 = 18, 7 = X, or null for unknown or unrated (adult). Parse order: null and the unrated spellings (NR, NOT RATED, UNRATED, PASSED, APPROVED, after cleaning) are null; clean by trimming U+0020 from both ends, upper-casing ASCII a-z only, turning U+2013 into a hyphen and dropping a leading 'RATED '; then the aliases GP = 2, PG13 = 3, XXX = 7, C = 5, M = 5, A = 6; then the rating.json ladder (descriptor suffixes included, Approved excluded); then a country prefix (code or name, then -, : or /), whose table registry is EMPTY in kid-hold-6, so it never matches; then a list split on '/' takes its highest level, null if any part is null (each part an alias, a ladder name or a bare age); then a bare minimum age of one or two ASCII digits with an optional single '+' rounds up: 0 is 0, 1-7 is 1, 8-10 is 2, 11-13 is 3, 14 is 4, 15-17 is 5, 18 and up is 6; anything else is null. Every non-US system is null (adult) until a licensed per-country table lands (#987): the MovieLabs cmr-ratings repository had no licence on 2026-11-20. 'tier': the tier of one item. Encoding: shape movie, episode (of a known series, given in 'series'), episodeNoSeriesId, or episodeSeriesUnknown (its series ID is not in the snapshot); 'rating', 'genres', 'tags' and 'studios' are the item's (missing lists are empty), and 'series' has 'rating', 'genres', 'tags', 'studios' likewise. The decisive rating is the item's own, except for an episode of a known series whose rating is present (kid-hold-5: not null, not only U+0020 spaces), which uses the series rating; an unknown series has none. Tags and genres are the item's and its known series', folded to ASCII lower case and matched exactly. Adult: a null level, an explicit tag (RatingTier.EXPLICIT_TAGS) or an adult genre (RatingTier.ADULT_GENRES), or a level above 4. Teen: level 3 or 4, or level up to 2 with a gore tag (RatingTier.GORE_TAGS). Kids: level 0 or 1, no gore tag, and made-for-kids evidence: a kids genre or kid studio (kid-hold-4 lists) on the item or its series, the animated-family signal (kid-hold-5), or a KidHold.KIDS_GENRES spelling as a tag on the item or its series. A children's rating alone is not evidence. Family: everything else at level 2 or below. For every tier row, KidHold.admits and GrownUpHold.dropsFromGrownUp are true exactly when the tier is kids. 'customCeiling': under kid-hold-6 every app channel's ceiling reads Approved as unrated: a custom channel outside the Kids band (policy custom) and a grown-up channel (grownUp) run rules-format rule 4 except that an item whose effective rating, or whose known series' non-empty rating, normalises to Approved fails every ceiling; with no ceiling everything passes. Policy open is the rules-format reference evaluation (rating.json, the pool vectors), where Approved keeps rank 0. Rows have shape movie or episode (of a known series with 'seriesRating'), 'rating' the item's own, 'ceiling' a ladder name or null. 'passesKidCeiling': a movie with the kids genre under a kid channel ceiling (null = none): its level must be at most the ceiling's level capped at 1. kid-hold-6 still passes kid-hold-5.json, and kid-hold-4.json's kidHold4 column; its grownUpHold3Drops column holds only for rows with a kid rating (level 0 or 1).",
